From af3417e0e1a7ececa8fa29381b731f493b4e063e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 13:53:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: vertex programs: ARL needs r0, TRUNC into ARL, MAD
 fusion

ARL (patch 0082) did not load the address register when its destination
was r1: a first index in r1 read slot 0, the same index in r0 worked.
NVIDIA's ARL always writes r0 (ARL r0.xy A=r0.xyxx); 0082 had taken
"the register it reads" for the rule, which held only where that
register happened to be r0 (idx-two hid it: its first index was 0).
A program with an ARL now reserves r0 (its temporaries start at r1);
the lane of the source is the lane of the address register and the
reads select it with address_register_select.

Peepholes, run before the labels are resolved, each one for
straight-line code and a value that is dead afterwards, and never on
precise instructions:
 - the three TRUNC instructions go when an ARL reads the truncated
   temporary (NVIDIA: MAD, ARL),
 - MUL + ADD becomes MAD (NVIDIA: mad-fuse, mad-fuse-neg),
 - the exact x * 0.0 + x pair that NIR leaves after a varying, self
   moves, the copy nir_to_tgsi puts in front of an ARL, two ARLs from
   one register become one ARL r0.xy, a MOV is propagated into the
   instruction that reads it, MOV out = t goes into the instruction
   that wrote t.

Instruction counts against NVIDIA's compiler on the same GLSL: mad-fuse
3/3, mad-fuse-neg 3/3, mad-keep 4/4, idx-attr 4/4, idx-two-attr 8/8,
idx-offset 8/9, idx-two 8/7, mad-chain 6/5, mad-fetch 4/3.

Checked on the tablet: grate-vertex-ops-test 84 of 84 PASS (75 after
0082), also with every peephole disabled; the other grate-*-test
programs give the same output as the merged 0083 build; a full glmark2
run prints no vertex message and its 49 fragment layouts are identical
(score 249 against 232, one run each).
